  "description": "Aspose.Page for .NET converts PostScript (PS), EPS and XPS documents to PDF and to raster images, and creates and edits XPS documents — with no third-party software and no native dependencies to install.\n\nRunning on Linux, macOS or in a container? Use the Aspose.Page.Drawing package instead: the same API on a fully managed graphics backend, with no System.Drawing.Common dependency.\n\nThe API helps developers create and edit XPS documents while working with canvas, paths and glyph elements. Utilize a set of primitives such as elliptical arcs, straight lines and Bezier curve segments to create vector graphics. Add default print tickets and preserve them in the XPS file, link print ticket objects among job, document and page, and work with Print Schema model classes for ticket manipulation. Gradients, visual brushes and transparency are supported.\n\nAspose.Page for .NET supports the PostScript language levels 1-3, with the exception of some font types. It saves PS and EPS files to PDF and converts PS, EPS and XPS documents to BMP, TIFF, JPEG and PNG, as well as to EMF, WMF and GIF.\n\nWritten entirely in C# and distributed as a single managed assembly. Targets .NET Framework 4.0 and later and .NET Standard 2.0 and later, so it runs in console applications, services, ASP.NET and ASP.NET Core apps, and on Azure. For Linux, macOS and container deployments use Aspose.Page.Drawing.",
